---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EditorialNote from '../components/EditorialNote.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';
const a = site.analytics;
const analyticsOn = a.approvedByArtist && a.domain;
---
<BaseLayout title="privacy" description="How GilesMcCrary.com handles contact details and visitor data.">
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <span class="label">privacy</span>
    <h1>privacy</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap prose">
    <EditorialNote>
      Please read and confirm this page before launch, particularly the retention period.
      It reflects how the site is built; it is not legal advice.
    </EditorialNote>

    <h2>contact form</h2>
    <p>
      When you use the contact form, the site collects your name, your email address, an
      optional subject, and your message. These are used only to read and reply to your
      message. They are not added to a mailing list, sold, or shared with anyone else.
    </p>
    <p>
      Form submissions are received and stored by the site’s host, Netlify, which also
      screens them for spam. As part of that screening, technical details such as your IP
      address and browser type may be processed. Messages are kept only as long as needed
      to respond, then deleted.
    </p>

    {site.field.open && (
      <>
        <h2>the field</h2>
        <p>
          If you choose to leave a piece in the field, only its letters, their arrangement,
          and the date are stored, so that the piece can be shown to others as part of the
          collective work. No name, email address, IP address, or other information about
          you is stored with it. The word you type is checked automatically for offensive
          language, links, and contact details, and is not kept separately.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your browser keeps a private key for each piece you leave, so that you, and only
          you, can remove it later from the piece's page. To limit abuse, the host counts
          how often pieces are submitted from the same connection for a short time; this is
          not stored with your piece. Pieces can be reported by visitors and are reviewed by
          the artist.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing you do in <a href="/abstract/">abstract your own concrete</a> leaves your
          browser unless you choose to leave it in the field.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>analytics</h2>
    {analyticsOn ? (
      <p>
        The site uses Plausible Analytics to count visits in aggregate (pages viewed,
        referring sites, country, device type). It sets no cookies and collects no
        personal information. No visitor is identified or tracked across other websites.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        The site does not currently use analytics, advertising, or tracking cookies.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2>cookies</h2>
    <p>
      The public site sets no cookies. If you leave a piece in the field, your browser stores
      a small private key (in local storage, not a cookie) so you can remove the piece later.
    </p>

    <h2>server logs</h2>
    <p>
      As with any website, the hosting provider keeps short-lived technical logs (such as
      IP addresses and requested pages) to deliver the site securely and reliably.
    </p>

    <h2>your choices</h2>
    <p>
      To ask what information is held about you, or to have a message you sent deleted,
      please get in touch through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>{site.contactEmail && <> or at <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a></>}.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
